We have 8 drones available. Field_1 has the highest threat (0.16) and requires 4 drones. We keep the 4 drones that are closest to Field_1 (Drone_1, Drone_5, Drone_6, Drone_7) on protection there so that this field remains fully covered. That leaves 4 drones (Drone_2, Drone_3, Drone_4, and Drone_8) for the remaining fields.

Both Field_3 and Field_4 have a threat level of 0.09. Field_3 needs 2 drones for full protection and Field_4 needs 3. With only 4 drones remaining we can fully protect one of these fields and leave the other partially protected. Since the rule is “always fully protect the field with the highest threat level using the closest drones” and Field_1 already takes priority, we now look at the remaining fields. Notice that Field_3 can be fully protected using the 2 drones (Drone_4 and Drone_8) that are nearby (each about 4 distance units from its center), while Field_4 already has 2 drones (Drone_2, very close with a distance of about 0.7, and Drone_3 with a moderate distance of about 7.5) even though it falls slightly short of its full requirement. In a situation where there aren’t enough drones to fully cover every field, it is best to guarantee full protection for one of the tied lower‐threat fields (here Field_3) by using the drones that are nearest to it rather than pulling one away from Field_3 (which would only make Field_3 partly protected) to fully cover Field_4.

Any drones not needed after protecting all higher‐threat fields may be left idle or assigned to a vacant field. In our case, the optimal assignment is to leave Field_1 and Field_3 as fully protected and let Field_4 remain with partial protection. Field_2 has a lower threat (0.07) and no drones are assigned there.

Thus, the final group assignment is as shown above.
