Explanation:
• The highest threat field is Field_3 (threat 0.10) and it needs only 2 drones. The two drones closest to its center (Drone_5 and Drone_7) are already protecting it so we leave them there.
• Next, among the remaining fields the ones with a threat level of 0.07 are Field_2 and Field_4. Field_4 already has the 3 drones needed (Drone_2, Drone_3, and Drone_8) so we keep that field fully protected.
• Field_2 however needs 4 drones but presently only has 3 (Drone_1, Drone_4, and Drone_6). With only 8 drones in the fleet (and no idle drone available), we choose not to disturb a fully secured field (Field_4) so we leave Field_2 with 3 drones.
• Field_1, with a lower threat (0.04) and requiring 4 drones, remains without any protection.
This assignment follows the rule: always fully protect the field with the highest threat (Field_3, then Field_4 because of proximity and full protection) and assign the closest available drones. The remaining drones (three on Field_2) are used on the field with the next highest bird activity—even though Field_2 remains one drone short, diverting a drone from a fully protected field would worsen the overall situation.
